00:01:14.172 --> 00:01:16.340
JEZ HUMBLE: So what is DevOps?

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.780
There's no agreed
definition of DevOps.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.050
I'm sure you have a
different one from me.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.600
Somehow mine hasn't
made it onto the slide.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.820
So I like to think
of DevOps in terms

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.320
of the goals we're
trying to achieve

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.760
and the people who are trying
to achieve those goals.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.420
So I like to talk about a
community of practice dedicated

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.120
to building, evolving,
and operating

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.600
rapidly changing, secure,
resilient systems at scale.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.760
So that's what
we're trying to do.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:41.420
It's hard.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:42.750
We haven't worked it out yet.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:46.160
We're all improving
all the time.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.789
There's three key areas that
I think we need to think about

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:51.580
and that our research
covers and that we're

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.000
going to talk about today.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.796
Number one is
organizational culture--

00:01:54.796 --> 00:01:56.420
we'll be spending a
lot of time talking

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.820
about that- technical
practices; and then,

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.980
finally, management
practices, which are also

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.500
important if you're going to
achieve the kind of things we

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.782
talk about.

00:02:05.782 --> 00:02:08.940
MELODY MECKFESSEL: So as
developers and operators,

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.960
we're really focused on
how do we take that idea

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.760
and get it out to
the user community

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.500
so that they can
get value out of it.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.480
And I think it's
interesting to think

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.650
about where we are in
terms of how DevOps

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.475
is enabling us to do that.

00:02:21.475 --> 00:02:25.211
So we have the idea of taking
that idea and understanding

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:26.960
what the requirements
are of the business,

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.580
like writing down
what features do

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.120
we think we need to actually
go fingers on the keyboard,

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.500
develop code around.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.990
And there used to be this
wall of throwing requirements

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:38.230
back and forth between the
business and development.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:42.080
Well, Agile really
helped us solve that wall

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:43.345
and break it down.

00:02:43.345 --> 00:02:46.220
But as we both know,
there's another wall

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.370
that has existed between
development and operations.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.936
So once the code
is done, it needs

00:02:52.936 --> 00:02:54.310
to be operated
out in production.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:57.350
And there's a wall that
exists of sort of throwing

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.090
the release over, so to speak.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:02.180
DevOps is helping us
break down that wall

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.930
and help increase the
velocity and the practices

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.750
around moving from
development-- writing,

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.580
building, and testing
code-- into operating

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.340
at scale from production
with the intent

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.740
that as we increase
that cycle time

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:19.970
and improve the reliability,
we're delivering better quality

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:25.200
results out to the market
and out to the users.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.960
So the research--
again, fascinating

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.690
to see the impact of DevOps
on overall performance.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.976
So software delivery
performance--

00:03:34.976 --> 00:03:36.600
there's some information
that comes out

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.580
around the difference
between high performers

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.380
and elite performers.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.740
And we see from your research
that elite performers

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:49.310
are 1.53 times more likely to
exceed their business outcomes.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.974
And those outcomes are
measured by productivity.

00:03:52.974 --> 00:03:54.390
They're measured
by profitability,

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.800
by market share-- real
concrete, positive measures

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.260
in terms of getting the
software out to the end

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:03.520
users and the market.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.672
It's fascinating
to see that impact.

00:04:05.672 --> 00:04:06.880
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.259
And I think that has been
one of the big outcomes from

00:04:09.259 --> 00:04:10.800
our research for
the last few years--

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.660
seeing that has a
business impact--

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.960
also just having a way to
measure DevOps' performance.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.450
So Google-- obviously,
very metrics focused.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.010
We found a valid
and reliable way

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.200
to measure our software
delivery performance, which

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.080
helps us also understand how
to change performance as well.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.120
We can do statistical
research on the factors

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.165
that improve performance.

00:04:28.165 --> 00:04:29.790
And again, we'll talk
about that today.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.430
So how do we
measure performance?

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:34.582
Two kinds of measures--

00:04:34.582 --> 00:04:36.540
measures of throughput--
how fast we're going--

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.450
so deploy frequency, how
frequently we deploy--

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.390
and then lead time for changes--
how fast can we go from version

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:43.920
control-- coding
version control--

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.075
to production.

00:04:45.075 --> 00:04:47.840
Then to stability
measures-- so time

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.880
to restore service if there's
an incident or an outage

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.526
or service degradation-- how
quickly can we restore service.

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:54.900
And then change
fail rate is kind

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.024
of a measure of the quality
of our release process.

00:04:57.024 --> 00:04:59.370
When you push out a change,
what percentage of the time

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.490
do we need to roll forward
or roll back and remediate.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.890
And if there's kind of one
thing to take away from this,

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.770
it's high performance
and not trading off

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:08.760
speed and stability.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.024
We kind of have this
idea that going fast-- it

00:05:11.024 --> 00:05:11.940
means breaking things.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:12.990
That's not the case.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.900
Consistently over five years,
we find that high performers are

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.700
better at throughput
and stability,

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.665
and low performers are worse
at throughput and stability.

00:05:19.665 --> 00:05:20.970
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
And it's interesting

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:21.980
when you think about it.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:23.730
You have to come back
and fix the problem.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:27.750
[LAUGHS] So you want to
decrease the amount of time

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.085
for that meantime to resolve.

00:05:29.085 --> 00:05:30.010
JEZ HUMBLE: Right.

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:30.510
Exactly.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:31.718
I mean, failure's inevitable.

00:05:31.718 --> 00:05:33.070
We're operating complex systems.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:33.975
You've got to take that
into consideration.

00:05:33.975 --> 00:05:35.325
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Absolutely.

00:05:35.325 --> 00:05:37.260
JEZ HUMBLE: So here's
what we found this year.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.720
Every year we do
cluster analysis

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:43.530
and we find groups
within our data

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.520
that behave in similar ways.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:48.240
This year, we
found four groups--

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:50.440
a low-performing
group that's releasing

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.040
pretty infrequently, between
once a week and once a month.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.290
Their lead time for changes
is measured in months.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.960
It takes them a long time
to restore service as well.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.990
And their change fail rate
is between 46% and 60%.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:02.550
So that's at the bottom end.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.770
Again-- doing badly at
all of those measures.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:07.290
And then up at the top end, we
have these elite performers,

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.600
that 7% of our total.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.010
They're deploying on demand.

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.022
So they have multiple
deployments per day.

00:06:13.022 --> 00:06:15.480
It takes them less than an hour
to get changes from version

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:16.824
control to production.

00:06:16.824 --> 00:06:18.990
If they need to remediate
because there's a problem,

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.610
they can do that in
less than an hour.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.230
And we think that's
because they're

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.709
able to use the normal process
for remediation as well.

00:06:24.709 --> 00:06:26.250
There's no special
emergency process,

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.600
where you skip a
bunch of the testing.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.310
You can just use
your regular process.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.101
And their change fail
rates are really low.

00:06:31.101 --> 00:06:35.040
They have a really high
quality release process.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.410
This year, we extended
our measure of performance

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:39.240
to also include availability.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.720
So availability-- obviously, new
users can access your service.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.030
And also, you can make
promises about the availability

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:46.960
of your systems--

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:50.070
so say that your users and your
customers can rely on them.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.470
Again, we find that people
are not making trade-offs.

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:55.449
They're not going faster, and
availability's going down.

00:06:55.449 --> 00:06:56.615
In fact, quite the opposite.

00:06:56.615 --> 00:07:00.000
Elite performers, in terms of
software delivery performance,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.395
are 3.55 times more likely
to have strong availability

00:07:03.395 --> 00:07:03.895
practices.

00:07:03.895 --> 00:07:07.425
So these things all go together.

00:07:07.425 --> 00:07:09.950
MELODY MECKFESSEL: So
let's talk a little bit

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:12.950
about that balancing
of speed with stability

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.500
because we know that
users' expectations

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.400
around availability
are increasing.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:21.490
So as an end user, if I go to
use a particular app or system,

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:22.940
I expect it to be up.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:25.440
I expect it to be available.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.950
So I just wanted to start
by grounding in the fact

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:33.170
that us as a DevOps community
and as software teams make

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:35.270
decisions around
availability-- and I

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:38.330
think we need to remember that
perfection is unrealistic.

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:40.730
As far as I know--
keep me honest here--

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:44.085
no human has ever created a
perfectly reliable system.

00:07:44.085 --> 00:07:47.150
And if we look out to nature,
we see the same thing--

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.750
competitive pressures,
evolution of systems.

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:53.300
Perfect reliability is
not the best target.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:56.040
So let's talk a
little bit about why.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.415
And I think it comes back to
perfection can be wasteful.

00:08:00.415 --> 00:08:03.530
So your users of your
application and your system

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:05.510
have many other dependencies.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:09.170
This is the reality with
infrastructure and cloud

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:10.370
infrastructure.

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:12.930
And your users
experience reliability

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.020
as all of those
dependencies together,

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:16.835
of which your service is one.

00:08:16.835 --> 00:08:19.190
So it's important to
come back to thinking

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:21.470
about the expectations
of your users

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:24.200
and the overall expectation
of those dependencies

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.180
and your understanding of them.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:27.624
They may not notice.

00:08:27.624 --> 00:08:29.540
I'm going to give an
example here in a second.

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:32.030
They may not notice
the difference

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:36.110
in adding two 9's to
your availability,

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:40.340
but they're probably
going to notice the cost.

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:44.059
So what is the goal around
setting these availability

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:45.440
and reliability targets?

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.420
It's really to make
our users happy.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:50.060
They expect this
level of availability.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:53.031
And we as DevOps teams have
to balance the features

00:08:53.031 --> 00:08:55.280
that we're giving to the
users with their expectations

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:56.480
of reliability.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.880
So we want to set those
reliability targets

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:00.980
to be in line with
the other dependencies

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:04.220
that the users have accepted
and to understand that.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:08.360
Insufficient reliability--
it makes unhappy users.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.910
Our goal is to make
our users happy.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:13.610
But if we have
excessive reliability,

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:15.830
we're going to
waste opportunities

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:18.890
to launch features and to
release at high velocity

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:19.820
as Jez mentioned.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:24.240
So think about the trade-off
that's being made there.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.650
And there's a bunch of
factors that can go into this.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:31.420
Users have different
expectations around service

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:33.580
levels that are set.

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:35.770
There's things that
we can consider around

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:40.280
expectations for paid
services versus free services.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:42.420
What's happening with
competitors in this market?

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.740
What are they setting
their reliability targets?

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:47.740
And always to come back
to the cost side of this.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:49.240
There's a human cost--

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.480
effort and time-- to hit
certain reliability targets

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:53.530
and availability targets.

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:55.870
So we need to consider
that in the decisions

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:58.110
that we make as DevOps teams.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:02.560
And at Google, we found that
this objective metric, called

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:06.930
an error budget, helps balance
availability with velocity.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:08.860
So you take 100% percent.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:10.900
You set your
availability target.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:13.180
And you have your
error budget so

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:17.320
that product management,
developers, and SRE are all

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:20.770
working off the same metric.

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:23.470
And there's flexibility
as we'll see.

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.540
If you have error
budget remaining,

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:28.360
maybe you are going to take
more risks on the release side,

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:31.090
having that understanding of
how your service is operating.

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:34.780
So this is an example.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:36.910
You can see here-- and
I just want to call out

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:39.910
the difference-- if you have
an availability target of three

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:43.240
9's, you have around--

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:47.260
you can see per year, per
quarter, 30 days in this slide.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:51.370
But you can see you have
about 2.16 hours of allowed

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:52.690
unavailability.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:54.022
So that's downtime.

00:10:54.022 --> 00:10:55.480
That's time that
your users are not

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:57.270
going to be able to
use your application.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:01.540
If you wanted to target five
9's, which, for some systems,

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:04.830
that may be correct, you've
only got 1.30 minutes.

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:05.649
I mean, that's--

00:11:05.649 --> 00:11:06.690
JEZ HUMBLE: --terrifying.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:08.750
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
It's terrifying.

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:12.805
So we really want to come back
to you can set this metric.

00:11:12.805 --> 00:11:14.680
Make sure it's in line
with the dependencies.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.840
Make sure it's in line
with the user expectations.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.570
And know what the
trade-offs are going

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:24.450
to be because that level
of service may be required,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.541
but it comes at a cost.

00:11:27.541 --> 00:11:32.400
So what are the benefits of
creating these error budgets?

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:34.700
We're going to talk about
culture in a little bit,

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:38.510
but creating common incentives
and a shared fate, or a shared

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:41.120
responsibility, around
uptime is so critical.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:46.070
And we found at Google that
between developers and SREs,

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:50.100
this is the way that we balance
innovation and reliability.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:54.110
And the other aspect of this
that I find really important

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:56.510
is that the developers
become self-policing.

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:59.300
So the operators-- you know
that wall between development

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:02.120
and operations that we talked
about-- just really break this

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:05.930
down, where everyone has a
common goal around uptime

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:08.201
and developers know
where those trade-offs

00:12:08.201 --> 00:12:09.950
can be made-- maybe
where we can take more

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:11.840
risks around future velocity.

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:13.330
But everyone's on the same page.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:15.710
Everyone's oriented
around that metric.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:18.950
And I have examples within
my development team,

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.892
where we were all looking
at the error budget

00:12:21.892 --> 00:12:23.600
and seeing where we
stand for the quarter

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:26.016
and making sure that we're
falling within our availability

00:12:26.016 --> 00:12:26.570
targets.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:28.460
Super important.

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:29.180
JEZ HUMBLE: OK.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:31.734
So we talked a bit about
what's important to measure

00:12:31.734 --> 00:12:33.650
and how you can think
about those measurements

00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:35.850
and use them to drive
changes in behavior.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.850
We're going to talk a bit
about how you actually

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:38.860
get better at this stuff.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:40.010
What are the things
that you can do

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:42.350
if you're not doing so well
in terms of performance that

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:44.060
can shift you to the
left in this case?

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:47.342
And shifting left is
going to be a big theme.

00:12:47.342 --> 00:12:48.800
So three things
we're going to talk

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.590
about and that we've measured
over the last five years--

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:56.770
technical practices,
culture, and management.

00:12:56.770 --> 00:13:00.809
So obviously, a lot of the focus
of DevOps is on automation.

00:13:00.809 --> 00:13:02.600
We're going to talk
about some of the tools

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:03.770
that you use at Google.

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:06.430
Automation is, obviously,
very important.

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:09.460
And in our data
this year, we find

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:12.940
that elite performers have
high levels of automation,

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:14.227
low levels of manual work.

00:13:14.227 --> 00:13:15.060
This is interesting.

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.351
This is something you can
only measure through surveys.

00:13:17.351 --> 00:13:19.690
You can't look at system
data and find out how much

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:21.340
manual work is being done.

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:23.560
You can only find
out by asking people.

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:26.047
We see the low performers are
doing much more manual work

00:13:26.047 --> 00:13:27.880
when they're doing
configuration management,

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:30.790
deployments, testing,
change approval process

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:34.420
than our elite
performers are doing.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:36.820
And that should be no surprise.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:39.010
One of the things that
elite performers do

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:42.100
is find ways to automate
things that are repetitive.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:44.080
They're algorithmic.

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:45.905
But these numbers are not 0.

00:13:45.905 --> 00:13:48.280
And it's important to bear in
mind that there will always

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:49.150
be manual work.

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:51.025
There's always going to
be things that you're

00:13:51.025 --> 00:13:52.390
going to have to do.

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:54.880
Continuous delivery--
there's this misconception

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.610
that some people have that it's
about automating everything,

00:13:57.610 --> 00:13:59.920
that we're going to get rid
of testers, for example.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:01.277
Absolutely not the case.

00:14:01.277 --> 00:14:02.860
There's still manual
work to be done--

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.410
exploratory testing,
usability testing--

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:07.516
that's critically important.

00:14:07.516 --> 00:14:09.310
The other thing to
point out about this

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:12.730
is that you can't just take
complex and painful processes

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:13.480
and automate them.

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:14.750
You have to simplify.

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:16.750
And so there's a
simplification piece

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.310
in terms of
architecture processes

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:22.030
that has to go hand in
hand with automation.

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:24.625
MELODY MECKFESSEL: You found
here that-- we talk about toil

00:14:24.625 --> 00:14:26.600
a lot, and there's many
dimensions of toil.

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:29.980
But you can clearly see how
automation is coming into play,

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:31.870
from low performers
to elite performers,

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:35.140
to really make time
to address the toil

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:38.242
and to factor those improvements
back into the workflow.

00:14:38.242 --> 00:14:38.950
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:40.741
And actually, it's not
in the presentation,

00:14:40.741 --> 00:14:43.205
but in the report, we
look at the amount of time

00:14:43.205 --> 00:14:44.830
that people are
spending on new feature

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:48.570
development versus non-value-add
work, like fixing problems.

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:50.160
And we find that
elite performers--

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:54.434
50% of their time is spent on
value-add work versus toil.

00:14:54.434 --> 00:14:56.350
So it's an interesting
reflection of something

00:14:56.350 --> 00:14:57.516
that you observed at Google.

00:14:57.516 --> 00:14:59.091
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Right.

00:14:59.091 --> 00:15:02.580
JEZ HUMBLE: So how
do you actually

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:05.052
improve your performance
from a technical perspective?

00:15:05.052 --> 00:15:07.260
Well, over the five years
that we've been doing this,

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:09.176
we found a whole bunch
of different practices.

00:15:09.176 --> 00:15:12.130
Obviously, there's the classic
continuous delivery practices,

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:14.760
good configuration management
and version control,

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:18.660
continuous integration,
automation around the test

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.180
and deploy process.

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:22.780
This year, we also looked
at continuous testing,

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:25.830
which is we're not just going
to automate some of our testing.

00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:28.980
We're also going to build
testing work into the software

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:33.180
delivery lifecycle, have testers
working alongside developers,

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:34.860
testing process.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:36.585
Monitoring and
observability-- turns out

00:15:36.585 --> 00:15:37.710
those things are important.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:39.960
Obviously, you spend a lot
of time thinking about that

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.100
and doing that at Google.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:42.990
So that has a big
impact on your ability

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:44.970
to deliver with
speed and stability,

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:48.870
building database management
and security into the delivery

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:49.380
process--

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:50.940
again, shifting
left on that stuff.

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:53.235
And we'll talk about security
in just a little bit.

00:15:53.235 --> 00:15:57.180
Those things together all impact
software delivery performance

00:15:57.180 --> 00:15:59.100
positively, and availability.

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:01.360
Interestingly, they also
positively impact culture.

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:02.901
So we'll talk about
culture in a bit.

00:16:02.901 --> 00:16:04.530
But how do you change culture?

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.500
Well, you change it by
changing how people work

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:08.529
and how they behave.

00:16:08.529 --> 00:16:10.320
Technical practices
are a big part of that.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.870
Also want to point out
these technical practices

00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:16.070
reduce deployment pain, and
they also reduce team burnout.

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:17.970
So obviously, we
want to avoid teams

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:21.750
that are working crazy hours
and feeling a lot of stress.

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:24.660
That's bad for the humans, and
that results in bad outcomes.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:27.180
These practices can
reduce burnout as well.

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:28.180
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:31.205
It's so important.

00:16:31.205 --> 00:16:35.400
So I wanted to share
an example that I

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:38.485
think demonstrates a lot of
the principles and findings

00:16:38.485 --> 00:16:39.110
that you found.

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:41.680
So Google invests
significantly in code review.

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:45.940
You can see a stat here-- about
20,000 code reviews a day.

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:50.174
We do this as a way to invest in
education among the developers

00:16:50.174 --> 00:16:52.090
and operators that are
writing code and trying

00:16:52.090 --> 00:16:53.980
to get those features
out to our end users.

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:57.000
But it's a significant
investment in quality.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.410
We want quality
code because we want

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:03.430
to find the issues before we're
further along in the deployment

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:04.839
pipeline.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:07.480
And we want to
find it definitely

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:09.010
before it gets out
to the end users

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:10.869
as much as we possibly can.

00:17:10.869 --> 00:17:14.230
So we've invested a lot in
automation around code reviews.

00:17:14.230 --> 00:17:18.910
And the intention behind this is
to have that automation free up

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:22.270
the humans' time to focus on
the content and the quality

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:25.069
and the improvements that
can be made to the code.

00:17:25.069 --> 00:17:26.420
So we've incorporated things.

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:29.600
We call them robo-reviews--
robot code reviews--

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:34.060
where we're looking for
checks and tests and analyzers

00:17:34.060 --> 00:17:36.040
that can do things
like enforce the style

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:38.969
and formatting because we know
that increases readability,

00:17:38.969 --> 00:17:40.510
and it makes it
easier for the humans

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:42.250
to interact with the code.

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:45.370
Compiler-based analyzers to
look for problematic patterns

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:47.800
in the code so that we
can prompt the developers

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:50.940
and operators to look at that
and take action based on it.

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:53.710
Custom analyzers-- we've
created an internal community,

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:55.930
an ecosystem, around
custom analyzers

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:58.030
that different engineering
teams can adopt.

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:00.700
And then if those are valuable,
other teams can adopt them.

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:04.900
So you're creating a community
around these types of analysis.

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:08.260
And then, finally, building
all of this-- build and test--

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:10.935
into the CI system
so that the code--

00:18:10.935 --> 00:18:12.310
we're running the
test, and we're

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.560
catching issues in the code.

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:17.080
And we're surfacing
those in the code reviews

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:18.880
as fast as we possibly
can to catch it

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:22.870
before it incurs the cost in
the rest of the development

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:23.470
workflow.

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:25.000
JEZ HUMBLE: And I
find it, I mean,

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:26.290
really fascinating
that you invested

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:28.040
so much effort into
making the code review

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:29.434
process smooth and easy.

00:18:29.434 --> 00:18:30.850
One of the things
we talk about is

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:33.310
working in small
batches, developers

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:36.750
being able to make small
changes and push them in.

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:39.310
That is critical-- to have
a code review process that

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:41.852
is simple and smooth so that
you can do that very frequently.

00:18:41.852 --> 00:18:43.976
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah,
I think there's definitely

00:18:43.976 --> 00:18:46.480
some interesting points there
around taking risk and knowing

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:49.510
that the automation is there and
then doing those small batches

00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:51.490
to be able to
release as frequently

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:52.750
as the teams possibly can.

00:18:52.750 --> 00:18:53.458
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:18:53.458 --> 00:18:55.010
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:58.430
So beyond code review, we
want to build automation

00:18:58.430 --> 00:19:00.644
into the entire workflow.

00:19:00.644 --> 00:19:02.810
And it's great because
developers and operators just

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:06.020
continue to raise the bar
on the level of automation.

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:08.390
Definitely shows up in
the elite performers

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:10.530
and how they're
spending their time.

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:12.980
So if we start, and we
just go around the circle,

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:14.540
we have Cloud
Source Repositories

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:17.840
to help with version
control, to present search,

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:20.180
and different levels of
insight into the code-- so

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:22.430
fingers on the
keyboard writing code.

00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:26.120
Then we go with 2, 3, and 4
into the build, test, and deploy

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:27.240
pipeline.

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:30.360
So Cloud Build-- we'll talk
about security in a second--

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.680
but the ability to build
and test your application.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:36.500
And then, using
Container Registry,

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:38.870
be able to then take it into
Spinnaker, an open source

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:42.770
deployment system, to
release your code--

00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:45.270
high-quality code-- to
the underlying platform

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:49.400
whether that's Compute Engine,
Kubernetes Engine, or App

00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:50.420
Engine.

00:19:50.420 --> 00:19:52.310
But we can't stop there.

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:54.860
So we have systems
like Stackdriver

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:57.740
that are monitoring,
alerting, and logging

00:19:57.740 --> 00:20:00.470
to look for if we
deployed something,

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:02.444
and there's an increased
rate of errors,

00:20:02.444 --> 00:20:04.110
we're going to make
sure we take a look.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.830
The operators can take a
look at that and take action.

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:10.070
And we actually have ways
to then bring that back

00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:12.620
to code that exists in the repo.

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:15.000
And then we go around
again to fix the issue.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:19.220
So I think this cycle of
continuous improvement

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:21.860
with insights and then
building that automation

00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:24.110
into the workflow really
makes the developers

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:26.540
and operators feel like these
systems have their back.

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:29.570
And then they're going to
focus on the high value

00:20:29.570 --> 00:20:32.220
content-related tasks that
they need to do with the code

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:33.125
and with the service.

00:20:33.125 --> 00:20:34.180
JEZ HUMBLE: And I
love the fact you've

00:20:34.180 --> 00:20:35.929
got all of these
feedback loops going back

00:20:35.929 --> 00:20:38.960
to developers from all the
different parts of the delivery

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:39.630
pipeline here.

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:40.463
That's really great.

00:20:40.463 --> 00:20:42.048
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Great.

00:20:42.048 --> 00:20:44.895
JEZ HUMBLE: So security--
finally, we made it.

00:20:44.895 --> 00:20:46.895
MELODY MECKFESSEL: We all
have to care about it.

00:20:46.895 --> 00:20:47.603
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:20:47.603 --> 00:20:49.130
I mean, this is
something I often

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:52.400
say-- that you can't build
stuff that doesn't have security

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:55.370
and performance and have
the magic DevOps pixies come

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:57.470
after dev complete and
wave their magic wand

00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:00.310
and fix the security and
performance and availability.

00:21:00.310 --> 00:21:01.790
You have to build it in.

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:05.060
So we find in our research--

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:07.270
building security into the
software delivery process,

00:21:07.270 --> 00:21:10.580
shifting left, improves not just
software delivery performance,

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:13.500
but also the quality
of your security.

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:15.790
So it improves both outcomes.

00:21:15.790 --> 00:21:17.540
When we gather the
data this year,

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:20.510
we find that elite performers
are building security in.

00:21:20.510 --> 00:21:23.780
And they can do security reviews
and remediate any problems

00:21:23.780 --> 00:21:26.660
they find in the space of days
whereas low performers are

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:29.397
taking weeks-- not just to
conduct security reviews,

00:21:29.397 --> 00:21:30.980
but also to fix the
problems that they

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:32.188
find when they're doing them.

00:21:32.188 --> 00:21:36.290
So that's a real problem and a
real constraint for many people

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:37.660
in their delivery process.

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:39.075
So what do we want to do?

00:21:39.075 --> 00:21:41.450
We want to build security--
and by running security tests

00:21:41.450 --> 00:21:42.590
as part of the
deployment pipeline,

00:21:42.590 --> 00:21:44.360
as you've talked about, and
I think you're going to talk

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:45.340
about a bit more--

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:47.160
and making it easy
for developers

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:49.454
to consume libraries that
are known to be good.

00:21:49.454 --> 00:21:51.620
Obviously, if there's a CVE
discovered in a library,

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:53.960
you want to be able to get
a fixed version of that

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:55.850
and get that out into
production as quickly

00:21:55.850 --> 00:21:56.932
as you possibly can--

00:21:56.932 --> 00:21:58.890
another instance where
time to restore services

00:21:58.890 --> 00:21:59.723
is really important.

00:21:59.723 --> 00:22:01.430
Lead times are really important.

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:07.550
Make sure that your
toolchains and your processes

00:22:07.550 --> 00:22:09.740
help developers build
security in as well.

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:11.350
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:13.910
The timeliness of security
reviews, the ability

00:22:13.910 --> 00:22:15.440
for the developers to react.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:18.500
And then where we go next is
building that automation in

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:20.060
to help them from the beginning.

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:21.530
So just some examples.

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:25.080
Again, Jez talked
about shifting left.

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:27.310
We're writing code, but
then we need to build it.

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:30.356
And as part of that
build, the code

00:22:30.356 --> 00:22:31.730
may be changed by
multiple people

00:22:31.730 --> 00:22:34.120
on the team, multiple
dependencies.

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:38.620
So we've built vulnerability
scanning into Cloud Build

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:41.600
to detect those issues
so that we know ahead

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:43.970
of time, before we get
further down in the deployment

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:45.590
pipeline, where
there's a problem.

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:49.130
We're also building in policy
creation and enforcement

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:52.730
to give the power and control
to the developers and operators,

00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:55.340
and to software teams,
to be able to enforce

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:58.250
certain policies, like
binary authorization.

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:00.170
So if you look at just
these two pieces--

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:01.940
and I think these are
really the beginning-- we're

00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:04.065
getting to the point where
we have something that's

00:23:04.065 --> 00:23:08.110
trusted that we can then deploy
and really understand that this

00:23:08.110 --> 00:23:11.540
is a high-quality release that's
going out because what we want

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:13.220
to know is if there's
something that's

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:16.220
untrusted, if there is a
security vulnerability,

00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:19.664
and empower the DevOps teams
to act on that before they

00:23:19.664 --> 00:23:20.830
get further in the pipeline.

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:23.150
So we're tightening that
loop around security

00:23:23.150 --> 00:23:25.472
to address those issues
long before it makes it

00:23:25.472 --> 00:23:26.180
to the end users.

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:26.690
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:27.210
And I know that--

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:28.670
I mean, security is so critical.

00:23:28.670 --> 00:23:31.240
For Google, you have
systems like email

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:34.850
that state actors are
trying to interfere with.

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:37.190
So this is a great example
of how, actually, these

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:39.860
practices really help
with improving security.

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:44.420
So management practices.

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:48.020
So management is important
as well, obviously.

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:50.870
Over the years, we've found
that lean approaches to product

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:53.980
management are really critical
at improving performance

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:56.330
and culture and those
organizational outcomes we

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:57.150
talked about--

00:23:57.150 --> 00:24:01.580
profitability, market share,
quality, customer satisfaction.

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:04.520
What we find is working
in small batches;

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:07.550
developing small-feature
increments, prototypes;

00:24:07.550 --> 00:24:09.710
pushing those out to
production; getting feedback

00:24:09.710 --> 00:24:11.840
from real users; and
then iterating rapidly

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:14.870
to build things that deliver
the outcomes you want

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:16.850
for your users and
your organization,

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:18.456
enabling teams to experiment.

00:24:18.456 --> 00:24:20.330
We're not throwing
requirements over the wall

00:24:20.330 --> 00:24:22.760
to order-taking
development teams.

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:26.137
Development teams actually have
input into what gets built.

00:24:26.137 --> 00:24:28.220
I mean, you have these
highly trained, experienced

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:29.553
professionals building software.

00:24:29.553 --> 00:24:32.180
They have ideas and
opinions, and it's crazy

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:33.860
not to leverage that.

00:24:33.860 --> 00:24:39.320
So we find that those practices
together positively impacts

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:43.242
culture, performance,
organizational performance.

00:24:43.242 --> 00:24:45.130
We talked about culture a lot.

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:47.670
I just want to quickly dive
into how we measured culture.

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:50.500
And again, Project
Aristotle or Google--

00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:53.380
we confirm some of the
findings from that as well.

00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:55.180
We have this model
of culture that

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:58.270
comes from a sociologist
called Ron Westrum.

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:01.462
He's looking at safety outcomes
in aviation and health care,

00:25:01.462 --> 00:25:02.545
so safety-critical fields.

00:25:02.545 --> 00:25:06.790
And he has this typology where
he divides organizations up as

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:09.490
to whether they're
pathological-- power oriented--

00:25:09.490 --> 00:25:12.730
bureaucratic; or generative,
or mission or performance

00:25:12.730 --> 00:25:14.110
oriented.

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:16.840
And you can probably, if you're
following along at home here,

00:25:16.840 --> 00:25:18.880
work out where you fit
on this pretty easily.

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:21.520
It's kind of quite
a visceral model.

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:24.610
And there's six different
axes here that he looks at--

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:26.350
the extent to which
people cooperate

00:25:26.350 --> 00:25:28.760
between different parts
of the organization.

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:30.940
How do we deal with people
who bring us bad news?

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:32.590
Do we train people
to bring us bad news

00:25:32.590 --> 00:25:35.770
so that we can act on it rapidly
before something really bad

00:25:35.770 --> 00:25:36.700
happens?

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:38.530
Do we ignore people
who bring us bad news?

00:25:38.530 --> 00:25:42.820
Or do we shoot the
messenger and say

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:45.400
how dare you tell me this
thing that I don't want anyone

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:46.769
to know about?

00:25:46.769 --> 00:25:48.310
How do we deal with
responsibilities?

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:49.690
Do we avoid them
because we know we're

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:51.460
going to get into trouble
when things go wrong?

00:25:51.460 --> 00:25:53.126
Or do we believe that
we succeed or fail

00:25:53.126 --> 00:25:56.594
as a team and share risks
because we trust each other?

00:25:56.594 --> 00:25:58.760
How do we deal with bridging
between different parts

00:25:58.760 --> 00:25:59.789
of the organization?

00:25:59.789 --> 00:26:02.080
And then two things that are
really two different sides

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:03.850
of the same coin, I think--

00:26:03.850 --> 00:26:06.760
how do we deal with failure
and the extent to which we're

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:07.870
able to innovate.

00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:12.080
So failure here-- does that
lead to you blaming people?

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:13.849
Melody, you messed this up.

00:26:13.849 --> 00:26:14.890
You're a terrible person.

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:17.260
Or we're building
complex systems.

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:18.280
Failure's inevitable.

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:20.260
Thank you for helping
us find this problem

00:26:20.260 --> 00:26:21.730
that we can now do
something about.

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:22.585
MELODY MECKFESSEL: That's right.

00:26:22.585 --> 00:26:25.376
JEZ HUMBLE: And that's important
if you're going to innovate.

00:26:25.376 --> 00:26:27.250
Innovate, by definition,
is doing things that

00:26:27.250 --> 00:26:28.430
have never been done before.

00:26:28.430 --> 00:26:29.860
So there's a high
risk of failure

00:26:29.860 --> 00:26:30.880
whenever you're innovating.

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:32.230
If people are
worried that they're

00:26:32.230 --> 00:26:34.021
going to get punished
when things go wrong,

00:26:34.021 --> 00:26:35.500
they're not going to innovate.

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:35.770
So--

00:26:35.770 --> 00:26:36.620
MELODY MECKFESSEL: --yeah.

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:36.970
Go ahead.

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:37.510
JEZ HUMBLE: No, please.

00:26:37.510 --> 00:26:39.884
MELODY MECKFESSEL: You bring
up so many important points.

00:26:39.884 --> 00:26:42.672
I mean, humans-- we're the
ones writing the software.

00:26:42.672 --> 00:26:43.630
We're writing the code.

00:26:43.630 --> 00:26:45.190
We're operating the service.

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:47.020
We have to trust each other.

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:49.930
So I think DevOps has been
extremely powerful in starting

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:53.140
to break down those
walls and take

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:56.140
the practices and the tools
and the automation behind it

00:26:56.140 --> 00:27:00.160
and really help to reinforce the
trust between the individuals

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:01.517
because this is software.

00:27:01.517 --> 00:27:02.725
It's not going to be perfect.

00:27:02.725 --> 00:27:03.880
There's going to be bugs.

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:05.050
There's going to be issues.

00:27:05.050 --> 00:27:08.007
How we work together
in those situations

00:27:08.007 --> 00:27:09.340
is such an important part of it.

00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:09.850
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:27:09.850 --> 00:27:11.849
And our research backs
up exactly what you said,

00:27:11.849 --> 00:27:14.527
which is that the way you impact
culture is through implementing

00:27:14.527 --> 00:27:16.360
these practices-- these
technical practices,

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:19.390
these management practices.

00:27:19.390 --> 00:27:22.640
So a couple of things
that we found this year

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:24.410
in terms of impacting culture.

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:26.570
We talked about the technical
management practices.

00:27:26.570 --> 00:27:28.070
We also found that
retrospectives

00:27:28.070 --> 00:27:29.060
are really important.

00:27:29.060 --> 00:27:32.120
Retrospectives, learning
reviews, postmortems--

00:27:32.120 --> 00:27:36.165
these kinds of activities
impact culture positively.

00:27:36.165 --> 00:27:38.410
They also help create
a climate for learning.

00:27:38.410 --> 00:27:40.360
A climate for learning
is when organizations

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:42.940
treat learning as an investment
or something that we should

00:27:42.940 --> 00:27:45.130
make sure people have the
resources and time to be

00:27:45.130 --> 00:27:47.129
able to do, not some
kind of afterthought.

00:27:47.129 --> 00:27:48.670
Well, do your learning
on the weekend

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:51.820
so that you can work the
number of hours you're supposed

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:53.810
to work in your time card.

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:57.730
So what we find is that elite
performers are 1.5 times more

00:27:57.730 --> 00:28:00.280
likely to consistently hold
retrospectives and learning

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:02.470
reviews and, also,
critically, to use

00:28:02.470 --> 00:28:06.880
the outputs of those activities
to improve their work.

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:08.615
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:28:08.615 --> 00:28:11.000
You bring up so many important
points from the research.

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:12.170
I just wanted to share.

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:15.491
At Google, we see a lot of
these principles in action.

00:28:15.491 --> 00:28:17.490
And I just wanted to
highlight a couple of them.

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:22.820
So the first part is how do you
take the personal out of it.

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:23.700
Look at the data.

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:24.440
Look at the code.

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:25.700
Look at the logs.

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:26.960
Look at the dependencies.

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:28.130
Look at traces.

00:28:28.130 --> 00:28:29.750
Understand error rates.

00:28:29.750 --> 00:28:33.140
Developers and operators
come back to the data

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:34.490
to guide those decisions.

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:36.890
How can we learn the
most that we possibly can

00:28:36.890 --> 00:28:40.310
about what's happening in the
system to fix the problem?

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:42.980
Having a shared fate
and collaborating

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:45.020
between developers
and operators.

00:28:45.020 --> 00:28:48.230
So we shared an example
today about error budgets

00:28:48.230 --> 00:28:51.890
and how developers and SREs work
together around error budgets.

00:28:51.890 --> 00:28:54.800
Getting everyone oriented
around what we're trying to do

00:28:54.800 --> 00:28:57.620
and why we're trying to do
it is such an important part.

00:28:57.620 --> 00:28:59.150
It affects communication
and affects

00:28:59.150 --> 00:29:01.640
trust and how we work together.

00:29:01.640 --> 00:29:02.600
Transparency.

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:04.370
So everyone sees the same thing.

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:09.530
We have tools in writing code,
in running build and test

00:29:09.530 --> 00:29:12.620
in our code review that we
highlighted a little bit today

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:15.170
that makes all
that data visible.

00:29:15.170 --> 00:29:17.330
So we're not
withholding information

00:29:17.330 --> 00:29:21.651
or trying to control information
in your cultural observations

00:29:21.651 --> 00:29:23.900
so that people can take
action to resolve the issue as

00:29:23.900 --> 00:29:24.980
quickly as possible.

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:27.350
And near and dear to my heart--

00:29:27.350 --> 00:29:28.340
blameless postmortems.

00:29:28.340 --> 00:29:32.240
My development team
does them every week.

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:33.680
We sit down.

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:34.370
We learn.

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:36.740
We talk about what happened.

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:38.690
We understand what
measures we took.

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:40.970
We think about not
just technical fixes

00:29:40.970 --> 00:29:42.440
but process fixes.

00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:44.210
And we take away a
set of to-dos that we

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:46.650
hold ourselves accountable for.

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:49.640
And again, this is
just reinforcing

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:51.499
that we're going to take risks.

00:29:51.499 --> 00:29:53.790
We're going to do things that
haven't been done before.

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:55.700
But we're going
to learn from it.

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:56.910
We're all here to learn.

00:29:56.910 --> 00:29:58.850
We all make mistakes.

00:29:58.850 --> 00:30:02.930
Perfection is, as I said
before, unrealistic.

00:30:02.930 --> 00:30:05.180
And we're building that
trust between the developers

00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:06.410
and operators by doing that.

00:30:06.410 --> 00:30:06.870
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:09.244
And I just want to put in a
quick plug for the Google SRE

00:30:09.244 --> 00:30:12.800
book and the Google SRE workbook
that just came out last month,

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:13.479
I believe.

00:30:13.479 --> 00:30:15.020
There's a lot of
discussion about how

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:17.134
you do those kinds of
activities at Google,

00:30:17.134 --> 00:30:18.050
which is really great.

00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:21.560
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Absolutely.

00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:24.290
JEZ HUMBLE: So open source.

00:30:24.290 --> 00:30:26.770
Obviously, open source
is a big deal at Google.

00:30:26.770 --> 00:30:28.120
You contribute a lot.

00:30:28.120 --> 00:30:31.510
We found that, actually,
only 58% of responders

00:30:31.510 --> 00:30:35.140
said they were using open-source
components, libraries,

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:36.716
and platforms extensively.

00:30:36.716 --> 00:30:37.840
That was a surprise for me.

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:40.294
I expected it to be more,
and I wonder the extent

00:30:40.294 --> 00:30:41.710
to which people
aren't considering

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:43.030
all the layers in the stack.

00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:44.984
I mean, it's kind of
inconceivable to me

00:30:44.984 --> 00:30:47.150
that anyone would not be
using open source somewhere

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:49.066
whether it's in terms
of the operating system,

00:30:49.066 --> 00:30:50.625
the libraries they
are consuming,

00:30:50.625 --> 00:30:52.920
and the other pieces of the
ecosystem infrastructure

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:54.105
and so forth.

00:30:54.105 --> 00:30:56.200
But this is what the data says.

00:30:56.200 --> 00:30:59.710
50% agreed that their
team planned to expand

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:01.230
use of open-source software.

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:04.390
And we find that it's correlated
with high performance.

00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:07.300
Elite performers
are 1.75 times more

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:11.690
likely to make extensive use
of open-source components.

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:14.110
MELODY MECKFESSEL: So at
Google Cloud Platform,

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:18.180
we're very committed to
investing in open source.

00:31:18.180 --> 00:31:21.550
Google has one of the
highest contribution

00:31:21.550 --> 00:31:22.630
rates to open source.

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:25.650
We believe in the power and
influence of open source.

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:27.550
And you can see
some examples here

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:30.490
of recent open-source
contributions.

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:32.830
Skaffold saw some
issues that existed

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:35.980
in the local build and test
and deploy workflow, wanted

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:39.730
to help influence and
create solutions there.

00:31:39.730 --> 00:31:43.450
Kubernetes-- we know that
the adoption of containerized

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:46.090
workflows is
significantly increasing--

00:31:46.090 --> 00:31:49.300
significant community there
in terms of contributions.

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:52.900
Istio and many others.

00:31:52.900 --> 00:31:56.770
When I talk to customers and
developers and operators,

00:31:56.770 --> 00:32:00.280
there's such power in being
able to contribute and influence

00:32:00.280 --> 00:32:02.740
how the open-source
product is evolving.

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:05.440
And the community
and power around that

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:08.110
is so significant for
enterprise customers

00:32:08.110 --> 00:32:09.670
not wanting to be locked in.

00:32:09.670 --> 00:32:12.484
We've noticed this change in
the last 5 to 10 years around

00:32:12.484 --> 00:32:13.900
and the acceptance
of open source.

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:17.032
And it's really
exciting to see that.

00:32:17.032 --> 00:32:18.490
JEZ HUMBLE: Google
Cloud Platform--

00:32:18.490 --> 00:32:20.230
you have a commitment to
open source as well, right?

00:32:20.230 --> 00:32:21.480
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yes, we do.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:23.235
We are committed to
being the open cloud.

00:32:23.235 --> 00:32:25.210
And that doesn't just
mean open source.

00:32:25.210 --> 00:32:29.350
It means open APIs and an
open platform for developers

00:32:29.350 --> 00:32:32.876
and partners and customers to
be able to collaborate together.

00:32:32.876 --> 00:32:35.680
So just to wrap up,
we've shared a lot

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:37.900
about DORA's latest
research report

00:32:37.900 --> 00:32:39.380
on high-performing teams.

00:32:39.380 --> 00:32:42.580
There's an opportunity
for us as software teams

00:32:42.580 --> 00:32:45.370
to use throughput and
stability and availability

00:32:45.370 --> 00:32:46.870
to measure our performance.

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:49.270
And we've seen the impact
of that in the research

00:32:49.270 --> 00:32:51.305
that Jez shared today.

00:32:51.305 --> 00:32:52.000
DevOps.

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:54.490
It matters whether it's
commercial or noncommercial.

00:32:54.490 --> 00:32:57.130
So we're seeing with the
advent of open source--

00:32:57.130 --> 00:33:00.670
we're seeing with enterprise
goals that are being set--

00:33:00.670 --> 00:33:04.060
DevOps practices are
starting to be employed more

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:06.220
so than I think they ever have.

00:33:06.220 --> 00:33:08.590
Gave you an example
of error budgets

00:33:08.590 --> 00:33:11.800
and tried to highlight how
that common metric can help

00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:14.590
reinforce availability
and a shared

00:33:14.590 --> 00:33:17.170
understanding of
availability and how

00:33:17.170 --> 00:33:23.110
to have both feature velocity
and stability and availability.

00:33:23.110 --> 00:33:25.390
And then with the right
set of technical management

00:33:25.390 --> 00:33:27.670
and cultural practices--

00:33:27.670 --> 00:33:29.950
can help DevOps,
help create trust

00:33:29.950 --> 00:33:32.800
among the humans that are
developing the software

00:33:32.800 --> 00:33:36.370
and increase velocity
and business outcomes

00:33:36.370 --> 00:33:37.210
for those teams.

00:33:37.210 --> 00:33:37.852
So-- go ahead.

00:33:37.852 --> 00:33:38.560
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:33:38.560 --> 00:33:39.130
I was just going to say--

00:33:39.130 --> 00:33:41.380
I mean, you only scratched
the surface of the report.

00:33:41.380 --> 00:33:43.475
If you want to go
download it, you can go

00:33:43.475 --> 00:33:48.580
to bit.ly/2018-devops-report.

00:33:48.580 --> 00:33:51.840
I think we'll put the URL
with the webcast as well.

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:54.210
But I mean, there's a
lot of material in there.

00:33:54.210 --> 00:33:56.710
We've just kind of highlighted
some of the key points today.

00:33:56.710 --> 00:33:57.876
MELODY MECKFESSEL: There is.

00:33:57.876 --> 00:33:59.770
I have to go back and
reread it another time.

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:02.030
So thank you so much,
Jez, for being here.

00:34:02.030 --> 00:34:05.401
Thank you so much for the
research that you provided

00:34:05.401 --> 00:34:07.525
and for sharing this all
with the DevOps community.

00:34:07.525 --> 00:34:08.960
JEZ HUMBLE: Thanks so
much for having me.

00:34:08.960 --> 00:34:10.168
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Thank you.

00:34:10.168 --> 00:34:12.820
So stay tuned for the live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:13.319
minute.

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:00.257
Thanks for coming back.

00:36:00.257 --> 00:36:02.090
We're here now to answer
some live questions

00:36:02.090 --> 00:36:02.840
from the audience.

00:36:02.840 --> 00:36:05.000
I'm Melody, and this is Jez.

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:05.902
So let's-- yeah.

00:36:05.902 --> 00:36:06.860
Let's just get into it.

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:07.485
JEZ HUMBLE: OK.

00:36:07.485 --> 00:36:08.285
So one question.

00:36:08.285 --> 00:36:10.340
Any nuggets of advice
to give to someone

00:36:10.340 --> 00:36:14.450
carrying out an Agile/DevOps
transformation at a huge bank?

00:36:14.450 --> 00:36:16.490
So I hear this a lot.

00:36:16.490 --> 00:36:18.886
We're regulated-- it's
financial services,

00:36:18.886 --> 00:36:22.010
working in government or
something-- and it's hard.

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:25.670
So I want to acknowledge,
yes, it is hard.

00:36:25.670 --> 00:36:28.694
However, we do find
in the research that's

00:36:28.694 --> 00:36:30.110
in the report this
year-- there is

00:36:30.110 --> 00:36:32.930
no difference, no statistically
significant difference,

00:36:32.930 --> 00:36:35.900
between verticals in terms of
your ability to do this stuff.

00:36:35.900 --> 00:36:41.270
We find regulated organizations,
large organizations

00:36:41.270 --> 00:36:43.520
in the high-performing and
the elite-performing group.

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:44.980
So you can do this.

00:36:44.980 --> 00:36:48.680
We've seen continuously
redone with mainframes.

00:36:48.680 --> 00:36:51.110
So it's hard.

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:55.910
I think the advice is probably
don't do the whole organization

00:36:55.910 --> 00:36:56.450
at once.

00:36:56.450 --> 00:36:57.866
Find some part of
the organization

00:36:57.866 --> 00:37:01.310
that you can practice on,
that you can start learning,

00:37:01.310 --> 00:37:03.200
and then take the
things that work

00:37:03.200 --> 00:37:05.240
and move them up through
the organization--

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:07.490
except that there's a bunch
of things that won't work.

00:37:07.490 --> 00:37:10.600
And that's OK because you're
learning as you go along.

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:14.270
So my advice is go
for it, but accept

00:37:14.270 --> 00:37:15.770
that it's going to
be hard, and it's

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:16.910
going to take a bunch of time.

00:37:16.910 --> 00:37:18.140
Don't let that stop
you from starting.

00:37:18.140 --> 00:37:19.639
MELODY MECKFESSEL:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:37:19.639 --> 00:37:23.570
When I talk to financial
institutions or institutions

00:37:23.570 --> 00:37:26.480
in health care, this
question keeps coming up.

00:37:26.480 --> 00:37:32.150
And I think my advice
would be similar to Jez.

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:35.930
Start with a small piece and
really gain some momentum

00:37:35.930 --> 00:37:38.210
with that, because we know--

00:37:38.210 --> 00:37:40.280
if you look out to open
source, look at some

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:42.050
of the research we've done--

00:37:42.050 --> 00:37:44.420
developers look to
other developers

00:37:44.420 --> 00:37:45.950
for help and guidance.

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:49.940
And when you have the success
on maybe a particular area

00:37:49.940 --> 00:37:55.040
of your pipeline, if you start
with CI/CD, it can go viral.

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:56.775
And other developers
will learn about it.

00:37:56.775 --> 00:37:58.150
They'll look to
it as an example.

00:37:58.150 --> 00:38:00.050
And then they will
help make it better.

00:38:00.050 --> 00:38:02.725
So start small, get
some momentum going.

00:38:02.725 --> 00:38:04.520
And then you can
really build from that

00:38:04.520 --> 00:38:07.694
because the culture
takes time to change.

00:38:07.694 --> 00:38:08.360
We've seen that.

00:38:08.360 --> 00:38:10.790
It takes reinforcement
of practices--

00:38:10.790 --> 00:38:13.460
takes development of
tools-- which takes time,

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:16.730
and that shift between how
the humans work together

00:38:16.730 --> 00:38:20.350
within whatever company it
is that you're working at.

00:38:20.350 --> 00:38:22.100
JEZ HUMBLE: And I think
huge organizations

00:38:22.100 --> 00:38:23.058
are very heterogeneous.

00:38:23.058 --> 00:38:25.139
You will typically find
in a big organization

00:38:25.139 --> 00:38:26.430
that all the answers are there.

00:38:26.430 --> 00:38:28.700
They're just scattered
around in all different parts

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:29.300
of the organization.

00:38:29.300 --> 00:38:31.300
So find the people who
are doing the good stuff,

00:38:31.300 --> 00:38:32.560
and find ways to spread that.

00:38:32.560 --> 00:38:33.395
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Right.

00:38:33.395 --> 00:38:33.895
Yeah.

00:38:33.895 --> 00:38:36.650
I have also advised internal
hackathons and developer

00:38:36.650 --> 00:38:39.566
community events within
financial institutions, where

00:38:39.566 --> 00:38:40.940
you bring the
developers together

00:38:40.940 --> 00:38:43.107
to talk about the ideas
that they have and some

00:38:43.107 --> 00:38:44.690
of the changes that
they want to make.

00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:46.565
JEZ HUMBLE: Internal
DevOps days conferences.

00:38:46.565 --> 00:38:48.450
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Exactly.

00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:51.300
So can you talk a bit more
about misguided performers?

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:53.415
That was something that
the report mentioned.

00:38:53.415 --> 00:38:54.430
JEZ HUMBLE: Yes.

00:38:54.430 --> 00:38:56.400
And someone asked about
outsourcing as well.

00:38:56.400 --> 00:38:57.983
And these kinds of
things are related.

00:38:57.983 --> 00:39:00.220
So when we did the
cluster analysis,

00:39:00.220 --> 00:39:03.150
we found this group
that is kind of

00:39:03.150 --> 00:39:04.740
similar to the low performers.

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:06.720
They're releasing
very infrequently,

00:39:06.720 --> 00:39:09.370
but their time to
restore is really long.

00:39:09.370 --> 00:39:11.044
It's like multiple
months to restore.

00:39:11.044 --> 00:39:12.960
And we kind of thought
there must be something

00:39:12.960 --> 00:39:14.910
weird going on with,
like, is the data right.

00:39:14.910 --> 00:39:16.240
We checked the data.

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:17.880
That's what it told us.

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:19.297
And if you look
in real life, you

00:39:19.297 --> 00:39:20.755
can see things like
this happening.

00:39:20.755 --> 00:39:24.510
So, I mean, healthcare.gov
was a good example, where it--

00:39:24.510 --> 00:39:26.940
I mean, they got service
somewhat restored

00:39:26.940 --> 00:39:29.485
quite in the space of days.

00:39:29.485 --> 00:39:31.110
But it took them
months to actually fix

00:39:31.110 --> 00:39:33.068
all the systemic problems
in the infrastructure

00:39:33.068 --> 00:39:35.585
and fully restore service to
the level that was expected

00:39:35.585 --> 00:39:37.650
and required of that service.

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:39.480
Security vulnerabilities.

00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:41.190
If someone hacks
into your system,

00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:45.510
it could easily take you months
to pull all that out and fix

00:39:45.510 --> 00:39:48.510
all those problems,
fix any data problems.

00:39:48.510 --> 00:39:51.790
So when you think
about it, that's real.

00:39:51.790 --> 00:39:53.820
And we see it
reflected in the data.

00:39:53.820 --> 00:39:57.060
The message is don't
assume that going slowly

00:39:57.060 --> 00:40:00.725
is going to save you because
if you go very slowly

00:40:00.725 --> 00:40:02.850
and release very infrequently
and think that you're

00:40:02.850 --> 00:40:05.190
going to prevent all
the problems by doing

00:40:05.190 --> 00:40:07.530
all this very careful
prevention work--

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:10.180
that's going to hit you when
something actually goes wrong

00:40:10.180 --> 00:40:12.180
because it's going to
really impact your ability

00:40:12.180 --> 00:40:13.580
to fix that rapidly.

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:15.804
So that's what the data says.

00:40:15.804 --> 00:40:17.220
We also found a
strong correlation

00:40:17.220 --> 00:40:20.650
between misguided performers
and functional outsourcing.

00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:24.600
So people who are outsourcing
either development or testing

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:25.350
or ops--

00:40:25.350 --> 00:40:29.430
these individual functions--
we find that elite performers

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:30.990
almost never do that.

00:40:30.990 --> 00:40:33.607
Low performers do
it much more often.

00:40:33.607 --> 00:40:35.190
And actually, our
misguided performers

00:40:35.190 --> 00:40:37.740
were the highest users of that
kind of functional outsourcing.

00:40:37.740 --> 00:40:38.280
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:40:38.280 --> 00:40:41.070
I think there's two things
that that makes me think of--

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:45.360
the small batches-- so how can
you control the amount waiting

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:46.920
too long between releases--

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:49.620
and having a fast iteration
cycle that happens.

00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:52.200
We found that to
be true at Google.

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:55.785
And if we go back to this cycle
of continuous improvement,

00:40:55.785 --> 00:40:58.950
where we're trying to add
in automation and insight,

00:40:58.950 --> 00:41:01.680
I could see how that outsourcing
would be a challenge.

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:03.600
Are people looking
at the same data?

00:41:03.600 --> 00:41:07.380
Are they having access to make
good decisions and exchange

00:41:07.380 --> 00:41:08.830
information between each other?

00:41:08.830 --> 00:41:10.320
JEZ HUMBLE: Can they
even talk to each other

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:12.630
and change the way they work
and collaborate effectively?

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:13.671
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Right.

00:41:13.671 --> 00:41:14.460
Right.

00:41:14.460 --> 00:41:17.250
Interesting one we would
probably both talk about--

00:41:17.250 --> 00:41:22.260
any ways in which one can drive
cultural change for DevOps?

00:41:22.260 --> 00:41:25.920
Well, we've mentioned
some of them.

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:29.670
I think one of my
principles to come back

00:41:29.670 --> 00:41:33.150
to within teams, or where
you run into obstacles,

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:35.310
is where does the trust
start to break down.

00:41:35.310 --> 00:41:38.340
And I think having
a strong culture

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:40.500
that includes some
of the principles

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:41.670
that we shared with today--

00:41:41.670 --> 00:41:43.650
they really have to be lived.

00:41:43.650 --> 00:41:47.340
And you can come
back to at Google,

00:41:47.340 --> 00:41:49.200
we can use postmortems
to look at where

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:52.170
did things start to break
down between the humans.

00:41:52.170 --> 00:41:55.290
Were we not-- was our piece
of data that was missing--

00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:57.930
did someone not have
access to that information?

00:41:57.930 --> 00:41:59.880
Did we not have a
common understanding

00:41:59.880 --> 00:42:02.160
around uptime, what uptime was?

00:42:02.160 --> 00:42:07.575
And how was the team
not kind of coming

00:42:07.575 --> 00:42:10.504
to the outage or the issue
that was coming up with all

00:42:10.504 --> 00:42:11.670
of that information in mind?

00:42:11.670 --> 00:42:14.880
So we all are responsible
for upholding culture.

00:42:14.880 --> 00:42:17.160
And when we see it
start to break down,

00:42:17.160 --> 00:42:19.830
we all have to raise our
hand and say what can we

00:42:19.830 --> 00:42:20.700
do better next time.

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:23.242
How can we help each other and
help build the trust back up?

00:42:23.242 --> 00:42:23.950
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:42:23.950 --> 00:42:26.290
And you have disaster recovery
testing exercises at Google.

00:42:26.290 --> 00:42:26.785
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:42:26.785 --> 00:42:27.510
DIRT testing.

00:42:27.510 --> 00:42:29.700
[LAUGHS] Yes.

00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:32.430
Where we deliberately
try and force things

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:34.110
to go down to see
where we might have

00:42:34.110 --> 00:42:36.844
gaps in our response
and our practices.

00:42:36.844 --> 00:42:38.010
Thanks for bringing that up.

00:42:38.010 --> 00:42:38.210
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:42:38.210 --> 00:42:40.418
And I saw Kripa Krishnan
talk about that in the past,

00:42:40.418 --> 00:42:41.691
and that was really amazing.

00:42:41.691 --> 00:42:42.690
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:44.220
Great practices within SRE.

00:42:44.220 --> 00:42:45.180
JEZ HUMBLE: OK.

00:42:45.180 --> 00:42:47.590
I think we're kind of out
of time here, pretty much.

00:42:47.590 --> 00:42:48.140
MELODY MECKFESSEL: Yeah.

00:42:48.140 --> 00:42:48.681
Went by fast.

00:42:48.681 --> 00:42:49.415
JEZ HUMBLE: Yeah.

00:42:49.415 --> 00:42:51.540
MELODY MECKFESSEL: I know
there are more questions.

00:42:51.540 --> 00:42:54.720
We will make sure they get
responded to on the platform.

00:42:54.720 --> 00:42:57.040
I wanted to say thank you to
everyone for coming today.

00:42:57.040 --> 00:42:59.940
Thank you so much to Jez
and the team at DORA.

00:42:59.940 --> 00:43:01.560
Stay tuned for
